truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * volume_resize(): test $snapname for truth, not defined(). PVE's base plugin
    and every native one that refuses a snapshot resize use truthiness
    (Plugin.pm:1368, BTRFSPlugin.pm:503, LvmThinPlugin.pm:362), so an empty
    string means "no snapshot" everywhere else in the stack while this guard
    treated it as one. Unreachable in practice - the only caller that passes
    $snapname is QemuServer.pm on volume-chain storages, and it passes a real
    name - but a guard whose semantics differ from the code it stands in for is
    wrong in whichever direction it diverges, and this one diverged silently.
    Found by writing the regression test the guard shipped without.
  * t/nvme/17-resize-snapshot-guard.t: the test +idk10 should have had. An audit
    deleted the guard's die() and all 348 tests stayed green. This one goes red:
    with the guard removed, 3 of its 6 assertions fail. Note assertion 1 passes
    either way - volume_resize dies regardless, just later, on the missing
    config - which is why assertions 2 and 3 pin down *why* it died. A test that
    only checked "it dies" would have been decorative.
  * t/nvme/16-api-version-clamp.t: fail instead of skipping when the plugin does
    not load. The probe treated every subprocess failure as "no PVE on this box"
    and sent the child's stderr to /dev/null, so a plugin with a syntax error, a
    missing module, or a die in api() reported skip_all / NOTESTS / exit 0.
    Verified by mutation: injecting an unresolvable `use` made the suite green.
    Now only the three PVE modules the plugin loads at startup earn a skip;
    anything else fails and prints the child's stderr. A new assertion covers
    the distinction itself.
  * Fix two comments that described the code wrongly. api() claimed PVE "only
    warns when the claim is merely below" the host's APIVER - it also dies below
    APIVER - APIAGE; the window is closed on both sides. And the header still
    advertised "PVE 9.x (APIVER 14)" after TESTED_APIVER went to 15.

 -- IDKmanager <gerencia@idkmanager.com>  Fri, 29 Aug 2026 00:35:00 -0500

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Declare storage API version 15 and honour its contract. PVE 9.2.4 ships
    APIVER 15 while the plugin declared 14, so every plugin load emitted
    "implementing an older storage API". The warning was cosmetic -- APIAGE 6
    puts the floor at 9 -- but it was covering two real defects.
  * volume_resize(): reject a snapshot target explicitly instead of resizing
    the live zvol. APIVER 15 added an optional $snapname argument
    (libpve-storage-perl 9.1.6: "the new optional 'snapname' parameter of
    volume_resize() and the new 'virtual-size' field returned by
    volume_snapshot_info()"). The signature captured (..., $new_size_bytes,
    @rest) and swallowed it, so a caller passing $snapname would have grown the
    live volume with no error at all. The path is unreachable today -- it needs
    'snapshot-as-volume-chain', which needs volume_qemu_snapshot_method eq
    'mixed', and this plugin is raw-only and reports 'storage' -- but
    unreachable by configuration is not the same as safe, and the failure mode
    was silent corruption. Snapshots here are native ZFS and immutable, so
    resizing one is refused with a clear message rather than emulated.
    volume_snapshot_info() already satisfies the contract that applies to it:
    the POD documents a separate, smaller shape for replication ('id' and
    'timestamp'), and 'virtual-size' is only consumed on volume-chain storages.
  * api(): clamp the declared version to the host's, fixing a regression that
    kept the plugin from loading at all on older PVE. The three-branch
    negotiation returned $TESTED_APIVER unclamped whenever the host reported
    below APIVER 11, and PVE aborts the load -- not warns -- when a plugin
    claims a version above the host's. On PVE that shipped APIVER 10 ("bump
    plugin APIVER (10) and APIAGE (1)") the plugin therefore refused to load,
    despite a header comment claiming PVE 8.x support. The mismatch is
    asymmetric: overclaiming costs the whole storage, underclaiming costs one
    log line. A plain min(host, tested) is correct on every version, including
    hosts newer than us, where PVE now reports our real version instead of a
    hardcoded 11. New test t/nvme/16-api-version-clamp.t covers APIVER 9 to 20;
    it fails 7 of 16 against the previous logic.
  * get_identity() is deliberately left unimplemented. It arrived in APIVER 14
    and PVE calls it without a version gate, but the base class implementation
    is a die() and only PBSPlugin overrides it -- dir, nfs, lvm, zfs and iscsi
    all behave the same way in stock PVE. Implementing it would mean inventing
    an identity format with no consumer.

 -- IDKmanager <gerencia@idkmanager.com>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 11:40:00 -0500

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Cap the NVMe-oF request size from inside the plugin (new tn_nvme_max_io_kb,
    default 1024 KiB). TrueNAS SCALE 25.10.4 exports NVMe/TCP with mdts=0
    because nvmet-tcp in the Linux kernel defines no .get_mdts (nvmet_rdma_ops
    does), so the initiator takes max_hw_sectors = UINT_MAX and merges requests
    up to 32 MiB. On the target, nvmet_tcp_map_data() needs an order-5/6 kmalloc
    for that request's SGL and fails it as NVME_SC_INTERNAL under memory
    pressure -- silently, because of __GFP_NOWARN, and with a *generic* status,
    which NVMe multipath does not fail over from. A 1 MiB request needs an
    order-0 allocation, which is why the failure was intermittent rather than
    deterministic. Measured 2026-08-27: 980 of ~1040 failed requests were
    exactly 32 MiB, and a page-cached copy lost ~98 GiB of a 2 TiB disk while
    qemu-img exited 0. Reported upstream as issue #96.
    - Applied in activate_volume() before the device carries I/O, and swept in
      activate_storage() on every poll, so a namespace revalidation (which
      resets max_sectors_kb to the kernel default) is repaired within one cycle.
    - Both the head and the path devices (nvmeXcYnZ) are capped: the bio-based
      head only splits requests, the path devices re-merge up to their own
      limit, so capping the head alone is cosmetic.
    - Fail-open in both call sites: a cap that cannot be written logs a warning
      and never blocks a VM start.
    - Set tn_nvme_max_io_kb to 0 to disable once the array declares an MDTS.
  * Warn once per boot and per storage when the target declares no MDTS
    (max_hw_sectors_kb = 2147483647), naming the cap that is standing in for it.
    The warning stops on its own the day the array declares a limit, which is
    the signal that the cap can be retired.
  * The periodic sweep logs a repair as a warning ("REPAIRED DRIFT"), not as an
    info line: in the sweep every device is already capped, so one that is not
    means something reset it, and a repair nobody sees is how the original data
    loss stayed invisible for two days. First-activation capping stays at info.

 -- IDKmanager <gerencia@idkmanager.com>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 11:15:00 -0500

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk8b) unstable; urgency=medium

  * iSCSI CHAP could not establish a single session against TrueNAS SCALE
    25.10; both fixes are also proposed upstream as PR #95:
    - Discovery now goes through iscsiadm discoverydb carrying the configured
      CHAP credentials. 25.10 enforces discovery-CHAP implicitly as soon as
      any iscsi auth group exists on the array (the per-portal
      discovery_authmethod knob is gone from the API), and the old one-shot
      discovery additionally reset the discoverydb record.
    - _iscsi_login_all: list all nodes and filter by IQN, tolerating the
      ",tpgt" suffix. `iscsiadm -m node -T <iqn>` prints the full node
      record, not the "portal,tpgt iqn" list the loop parsed, so the main
      login path was dead code and every session silently fell through to
      the unauthenticated fallback. The retry fallback is CHAP-aware now.
  * Package version now states the tree's full identity. No functional
    change beyond the CHAP fixes: the resilience series described in the
    previous entry already shipped in the +idk6 package.

 -- IDKmanager <gerencia@idkmanager.com>  Sun, 24 Aug 2026 09:30:00 -0500

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk6) unstable; urgency=medium

  Everything here came out of one fault injection: tools/i32-apiloss.sh, which
  blacks out the array's management API at the packet level while leaving the
  fabric and guest I/O alone. Four assertions, all four failing when it was
  first run.

  * list_images_nvme no longer answers "this storage is empty" when what
    happened is "I could not ask". Measured: a storage holding two volumes
    listed as zero with exit code 0, three times, in 155s, 90s and 35s - not a
    timeout decaying into a partial answer, a confident wrong answer that is
    sometimes fast. Reconciliation is built on this call, and an empty list
    tells it every dataset on the array is an orphan. Three doors led there -
    the subsystem query dying, the subsystem not being found, and the
    namespace query dying inside `eval {...} // []`, silently. All three now
    die naming the question that could not be asked. The pool.dataset.query
    batch fetch stays non-fatal on purpose: it has a per-volume fallback, and
    11-list-fail-closed.t pins that so a later tightening does not turn a
    working degraded mode into an outage.

  * _retry_with_backoff counts time, not just attempts. It counted attempts
    and never looked at the clock, so the worst case was a product nobody
    multiplied: with tn_api_retry_max 5, six attempts of ~30s through the
    broker plus 2+4+8+16+32s of backoff is ~150s for ONE call, and against a
    direct connection where an attempt costs a full TCP connect timeout,
    closer to fourteen minutes. tn_api_budget_s (default 120) bounds the
    sequence, and the transport now asks for the time that is left rather than
    a fresh full window every attempt - without that second half the last
    attempt overshoots by a whole broker timeout.

  * cluster_lock_storage bounds how long the work holds the lock, not just how
    long we wait to get it. tn_storage_lock_timeout is the acquire timeout;
    once acquired the callback ran with no ceiling, so an operation making
    several calls held the lock for the sum of them. tn_op_budget_s (default:
    tn_storage_lock_timeout) starts its clock when the operation gets the
    lock, not while it queues behind another node. Measured effect: pvesm
    alloc went from still running at 180s to failing cleanly at 151s with the
    reason named.

  * status() stops asking an array that just said no. Both its lookups already
    ran with retry_max => 0, but one probe against a dead API still costs a
    broker deadline, times every truenasplugin storage, inside the single
    process that reports metrics for all of them. tn_status_budget_s (default
    8) bounds the probe - nobody waits on a capacity figure, and a stale
    metric costs less than a blocked pvestatd - and tn_status_probe_backoff_s
    (default 30) answers from memory after a connectivity failure. The marker
    is narrow on purpose: armed only by connectivity errors, never by a
    missing dataset or a rejected key, and dropped the moment the array
    answers.

  KNOWN, and not fixed here: the first call issued after the API disappears is
  blocked in a read on a socket that was already established, and no budget
  can interrupt a syscall already in flight - measured at over 180s where
  later calls settle at ~36s. pvestatd still stalls during that first window.
  And pvesm status computes every storage before filtering, so one slow
  storage darkens the node's management plane no matter what this plugin
  does. Guests keep running, quorum holds, and everything recovers unattended.

  Tests: 15 files, 291 assertions. Each new file was run against the unfixed
  plugin first; the tagged assertions fail there, which is the only thing that
  makes them worth having.

 -- IDKmanager <gerencia@idkmanager.com>  Tue, 18 Aug 2026 11:20:00 -0500

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * path() resolves the name and stops looking for the device. The nvme-tcp
    branch used to connect to the subsystem and run discovery, and it could
    die - so `qm destroy` failed, because PVE resolves path() on the destroy
    path and never calls activate_volume there. path() is name resolution and
    must not fail; ZFSPoolPlugin builds /dev/zvol/<pool>/<name> unchecked and
    LVM-thin hands back an inactive LV. Discovery now lives only in
    activate_volume, which is the authoritative activation point.
    Consequence, deliberate: destroy no longer aborts when the array is
    unreachable, so a zvol can be left behind. That is the same trade the
    in-tree plugins make.

  * The udev link is waited for where activation happens, not where the name
    is built, and it is required to be a block device. `-e` is true for a
    regular file, and qemu-img dd/convert creates its output - so a lost udev
    race could leave a regular file squatting the by-id name with the data in
    page cache. A squatter is now named and refused.

  * activate: say what the failure is when the volume was already freed. The
    hint is attached to _nvme_device_for_uuid, not to activate_volume, because
    measured against a live `qm destroy` the latter was never reached.

 -- IDKmanager <gerencia@idkmanager.com>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 19:00:00 -0500

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Do not destroy a zvol whose export could not be removed.
    _nvme_delete_namespace turned every nvmet.namespace.delete failure into a
    log line and returned normally, so the caller's success check was true
    whenever the query had worked, however many deletes had failed - and the
    dataset was then destroyed with force => true while a namespace still
    pointed at it. A denied permission, a timeout or a transport blip was
    enough, and the log line was level 1, which tn_debug's default of 0
    discards. Silent as well as destructive.

  * Make the guards on the deletion path fail closed. The linked-clone check
    on a base image, the active-namespace count that decides whether to
    disconnect the whole subsystem, and the free-disk-name listing all
    decided "it is safe to proceed" from lookups that had not succeeded.

  * Report an abandoned deletion wait as what it is. A job that outlived its
    window was classified as a hard failure though the destroy routinely
    completes afterwards; the dataset is re-queried before anything is
    reported, and the delete is never blindly re-issued.

  * create_base: the rewire rollback restores device_path, as the snapshot
    rollback beside it always did; pool.dataset.rename and pool.snapshot.create
    no longer retry, since neither survives being replayed; and a rename or
    snapshot whose response was lost is reconciled by asking the pool rather
    than by reading the error text.

  * Bound the connect to the broker socket. AF_UNIX does not refuse on a full
    backlog, it sleeps - and with a blocking socket, forever. Measured against
    the daemon with Listen => 32, stopped: connections 1 to 33 returned in
    0.00 s and the 34th never returned. The plugin's own deadline is
    established after the socket exists, so it did not cover this.

  * Keep status() off pvestatd's critical path. The pool-health lookup ran
    with the default three retries inside a function pvestatd calls every 10
    seconds for every storage, serially - around two minutes during which
    every storage on the node goes stale. The capacity cache could never hit:
    a 10 second TTL against a 10 second poll, tested as a strict age < ttl.

  * _wait_for_job_completion counted only the time it spent sleeping, so a
    "30 second" wait could run for a quarter of an hour while a task worker
    held the cluster storage lock. The deadline is wall clock now.

  * The retry backoff called sleep() with a fractional delay, which the
    builtin truncates to whole seconds - so tn_api_retry_delay below 1 gave a
    retry loop with no delay at all. Measured: sleep(0.5) returns in 0.000s.

  * Fix the bulk helpers, which passed four arguments to a three-argument
    signature and so died before reaching TrueNAS (#77).

 -- IDKmanager <gerencia@idkmanager.com>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 23:05:00 -0500

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * broker: bound every upstream read, so a TrueNAS that stops answering
    cannot take the daemon down with it. The socket constructors set
    Timeout => 15, which covers connect() and nothing else; every read was a
    bare blocking sysread. An upstream that accepted the TCP connection and
    then went silent WITHOUT closing it - a reboot, a fabric blip, or a
    firewall forgetting the flow all look like this from the initiator - left
    ws_read_exact waiting with no deadline. Because the daemon serves clients
    inline on one thread, that was not a single stalled request: the broker
    stopped answering anyone, permanently, and systemd's Restart=on-failure
    never fired because the process was still alive. Reproduced before fixing:
    first call 0.00 s, upstream goes mute, every call after it times out
    including calls from processes started afterwards, still wedged minutes
    later, nothing in the log.

  * broker: one budget for the whole request. Probe, reconnect, authenticate
    and call now draw from a single deadline set when the request arrives.
    Threading a relative timeout instead let each step start its own clock: a
    5 s liveness probe followed by a 30 s reconnect produced a 35 s request
    against a client that waits 30 s, so the client always gave up first and
    the work was thrown away.

  * broker: answer inside the client's own deadline. The client now states its
    budget in the request envelope and the broker replies a margin before it
    expires. With both sides on 30 s the client won the race by hundredths of
    a second, so the operator saw a generic "read timeout" instead of the
    broker's account of what actually failed. Clients that send no timeout
    keep the previous behaviour.

  * broker: distinguish an upstream that never answered from one that rejected
    the API key. Both used to die with "TrueNAS authentication failed", which
    sends the reader to check credentials when the fault is the network.

  * broker: TCP keepalive on the upstream socket, so a peer that vanished
    without a FIN is eventually torn down by the kernel rather than handed
    back out of the connection pool as though it were live.

  * broker: the run directory and log path may be overridden by environment,
    which lets the daemon be exercised by an unprivileged test. The systemd
    unit sets neither, so an installed system is unaffected.

  * t/broker/01-upstream-deadline.t: regression coverage for the above, with a
    local upstream that can be told to go quiet while holding the socket open.
    Requires no privileges and no TrueNAS.

  * t/broker/02-tls-short-write.t: the short-write case over TLS, which is
    where it actually happens - and which the plain-ws test in 01 cannot
    reach, because over plain TCP a large syswrite to a draining peer takes
    the whole buffer in one call.

  * broker: bound the CLIENT read too, and cap it. Bounding the upstream and
    not this left the daemon just as wedgeable from the near side: measured
    against that build, a local client that connected and sent a request
    without its trailing newline stopped the accept loop dead, and a healthy
    client behind it went unanswered for as long as it waited. A client that
    dies is harmless - the socket reports EOF - but one that merely stops, is
    stopped, or is blocked in D state on the very storage this daemon serves,
    is not.

  * broker: write the whole buffer, everywhere. syswrite is not obliged to
    take it all and on TLS it does not - measured on IO::Socket::SSL 2.085,
    8 KB and 16 KB went out whole while 64 KB and 256 KB both returned 16384.
    The daemon called syswrite once and discarded the count, so any larger
    frame went out truncated and the far end waited for the rest of a frame
    that was never coming; the request then died on its own deadline blaming
    the network. The same short write happens on a Unix socket once the reader
    stops draining, which is the reply path.

  * broker: SO_RCVTIMEO and SO_SNDTIMEO on the upstream socket. The
    select-based deadlines cannot see everything on TLS: select fires on
    ciphertext and SSL_read then needs a whole record, so a connection cut in
    the middle of one can still block inside a single sysread. The kernel
    bounds that regardless of what the SSL layer is doing.

  * broker: a timed-out connection no longer goes back into the pool. Only the
    die path evicted; a read that merely gave up left a connection of unknown
    state - possibly mid-frame - for the next request to inherit, and charged
    every later request the probe timeout for the whole outage.

  * broker: mask the pong. RFC 6455 requires every frame a client sends to be
    masked and obliges the server to fail the connection on an unmasked one -
    and a dropped session means a fresh login, which is the rate-limit spiral
    this daemon exists to prevent.

  * broker: cap the declared frame length. Now that a read can expire part-way
    through a payload the stream can resume mis-aligned, and sysread pre-grows
    its target to whatever length it is handed, so a bogus 64-bit value is an
    immediate out-of-memory abort - on a hypervisor, with the OOM killer
    choosing the largest process on the node.

  * broker: report a rate-limit lockout as one. It arrives as a structured
    error just as a bad key does, and calling it a rejected key sends someone
    rotating credentials in the middle of an outage that has nothing to do
    with credentials.

  * Fix Socket::gethostbyname, which does not exist. Verified on the Perl a
    PVE node actually runs (5.38.2): the package-qualified call falls into
    Socket's constant AUTOLOAD and dies with "Usage: Socket::AUTOLOAD()",
    while the core builtin returns normally. _host_ipv4 returns early for an
    IPv4 literal, so this only ever fired for a host configured by name - and
    then it failed every connection attempt. Present in both the plugin and
    the broker.

  * Declare tn_broker_timeout in properties() and options(). It was read in
    three places and declared in neither, and PVE rejects undeclared keys, so
    the timeout it documents could not actually be set. The broker now honours
    a client budget in both directions rather than only downwards, so raising
    it on the plugin side is enough on its own.

 -- IDKmanager <gerencia@idkmanager.com>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 21:20:00 -0500

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe-oF: identify a namespace by its UUID, not by a recyclable NSID. The
    selector matched on NGUID and, failing that, on the bare NSID - which the
    target reuses, so a deleted-and-recreated namespace can hand back a device
    belonging to another volume, and nothing downstream re-checks it. Measured
    against a live target: an all-zero device_nguid means the sysfs nguid
    attribute is absent entirely, while uuid is always present, and an all-zero
    NGUID passes the plugin's format check - so a namespace created outside the
    plugin took the unguarded NSID path. All zeros is now the "no identity"
    sentinel it is, the namespace UUID is a matching tier of its own ahead of
    NSID, and a device carrying a foreign UUID disqualifies an NSID match.

