truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1+idk1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Internal build: alpha + upstream PR #83 (NVMe-oF host whitelist and
    DH-HMAC-CHAP) + the portal reconciliation work. Not an upstream offer.

  * NVMe-oF whitelist: close the subsystem before authorizing the host, not
    after. The target holds allow_any_host and an explicit host list as
    mutually exclusive - linking a host into a subsystem that is still open is
    refused with EINVAL, and so is reopening one that has hosts. Registering
    first, which reads as the safe order, asks for the one state the target
    cannot render, and that aborted render is the failure this feature exists
    to fix. The window with the subsystem closed and nobody yet authorized
    cannot be designed away; it is survivable because the host ACL is evaluated
    at connect time, so controllers already established ride through it.

  * NVMe-oF whitelist: key the reconcile cache per subsystem, not per server.
    Two storages against one TrueNAS share a cache key, so a reconcile on one
    sealed the cache for a subsystem that had never been touched - while the
    configfs resync closed that subsystem by a path the cache never sees.

  * NVMe-oF: the configfs resync writes back the value the subsystem already
    has, never the configured one. It is called from four sites that know
    nothing about the access model: writing the configured value closed
    subsystems nobody had been authorized on, and against a subsystem hardened
    by hand it wrote true over an explicit host list - which the kernel
    refuses, aborting the very render the call exists to force.

  * NVMe-oF: report a half-applied whitelist at a level that survives the
    default tn_debug of 0, and treat a failed DH-CHAP key update as fatal
    rather than warning past it into sealing a success cache.

  * NVMe-oF: recognise what TrueNAS actually returns for a uniqueness
    violation. _api_call_mutate re-sends a mutation whose reply was lost, so a
    create that already succeeded is retried as a matter of course; matching
    only /already exists/ turned that routine duplicate into a die.

  * Keep TESTED_APIVER at 14. PR #83 bumped it to 15 in a commit that changes
    nothing else - no new method, no changed signature - so the higher number
    would assert a validation nobody performed.

  * NVMe-oF: tell a target that refused us apart from a portal that is down.
    Measured against a live nvmet target: a host missing from the subsystem's
    allowed list, and an NQN the target does not have, both fail the fabrics
    write with EIO in about 10ms, while a blackholed portal and an unreachable
    address both time out after about 3s. Reported as an ordinary connect
    failure, a locked-out node was indistinguishable from a dead fabric, and it
    also invalidated the portal-sync cache - putting the plugin to work
    rebuilding ports over an access list.

  * NVMe-oF: a node the target refuses now recovers on its own. status() calls
    _nvme_connect and never _nvme_ensure_subsystem, and registering this node's
    host NQN is something only _ensure does, so a node that was not authorized
    when the whitelist went on stayed out until someone started a VM on it.
    Repair mode now registers the node once on a refusal and re-reads the
    kernel's view.

  * t/nvme/02-host-whitelist.t: 27 offline tests for the whitelist. Eleven of
    them fail against the code as it stood before these changes. t/nvme/01
    grows 8 more for the refusal classification.

 -- Alfonso Kuen <alfonsok@gmail.com>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 23:55:00 +0000

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.24~alpha1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe/TCP: restore missing portals instead of stopping at the first live
    path. _nvme_connect() returned as soon as any live controller existed for
    the subsystem, so with more than one portal configured, a portal that was
    down during the initial connect was never re-added while another stayed
    up: the node ran on a single path indefinitely with nothing reporting it.
    Reconciliation now runs from status(), on pvestatd's timer, so it can
    never delay a VM start; the eleven hot call sites keep the original cheap
    behaviour and, while any path is live, only report a missing portal
    instead of reconnecting it.
  * NVMe/TCP: controller state is read from sysfs rather than parsed out of
    `nvme list-subsys`. The subsystem is matched with eq against each
    controller's subsysnqn, so "...:pve" no longer claims the controllers of
    "...:pve-backup" - with a common prefix that silently reproduced the bug
    above. No process is forked on the healthy path.
  * NVMe/TCP: new tn_nvme_ctrl_loss_tmo, tn_nvme_reconnect_delay and
    tn_nvme_keep_alive_tmo options, all optional and unset by default. With
    tn_nvme_ctrl_loss_tmo -1 the kernel maintains paths itself; the kernel
    default of 600 otherwise removes a controller after a ten-minute outage.
    A value of 0 is rejected in check_config: it removes the controller on the
    first error without retrying, which is worse than the default it overrides.
  * NVMe/TCP: _nvme_find_controllers_for_subsystem() compared the raw
    configured host against the numeric traddr sysfs reports, so a storage
    configured by hostname matched nothing and the function returned an empty
    list. It now uses the same key machinery as the reconciler.
  * NVMe/TCP: canonicalise IPv4 as well as IPv6 when building portal keys; a
    config entry such as 192.000.002.010 previously keyed differently from the
    identical address in sysfs, leaving a phantom portal reconnected on every
    poll.
  * NVMe/TCP: anchor traddr= with a word boundary so host_traddr= is not
    matched as the target address.
  * NVMe/TCP: a hard timeout on `nvme connect`, and udevadm settle is only
    paid when something actually attached.
  * _nvme_sync_portals() stamped its cache TTL even when a port create had
    failed, hiding the failure for a full window - exactly when the initiator
    is retrying a portal the target has not published yet.
  * Empty portal entries (a trailing comma in tn_portals) and ports with
    leading zeros no longer reach the untainters and abort the connect.
  * Repair mode always honours the per-portal backoff, including when nothing
    is live at all. Bypassing it there meant a target that was entirely down had
    every status() poll attempt every portal, each bounded by the connect
    timeout, indefinitely - stalling pvestatd for every storage on the node. The
    hot path keeps the bypass, since that call decides whether a VM gets a disk.
  * A portal configured by name that cannot currently be resolved is tracked
    separately from one whose controller the kernel is retrying. It cannot be
    matched against the numeric traddr sysfs reports, so treating it as absent
    fired a connect at a healthy subsystem from the hot path - but treating it
    as recovering was worse, since with no controller at all the reconciler then
    neither connected nor failed, turning a total outage into a silent success.
    _nvme_connect() now fails when the subsystem has no controller whatsoever,
    whatever the portals resolve to, which the callers that are not wrapped in
    eval depend on.
  * The hot path no longer resolves names at all: it answers from the memo or
    treats the portal as unknown. getaddrinfo is synchronous, and a wedged
    resolver costs more than the connect this code deliberately refuses to make
    there - and resolver failures correlate with the outage being diagnosed.
    Failed lookups are memoized for 10s rather than the full 300s.
  * The redundancy count is taken per configured portal, matching its
    denominator. Counting every live controller of the subsystem instead
    included ones belonging to a portal since removed from tn_portals, and
    reported two of two with a single usable path.
  * Portal addresses with a leading-zero octet are rejected in check_config,
    in NVMe/TCP mode only so that existing iSCSI configurations keep parsing.
    This plugin normalises 192.000.002.010 to 192.0.2.10 for key matching while
    nvme connect hands the raw string to inet_aton, where 010 is octal eight - a
    different host, whose controller could never match its own configuration.
  * The reduced-redundancy warnings are emitted at log level 0. tn_debug
    defaults to 0 and _log drops anything above it, so a level 1 warning was
    invisible on a default install - which would have left the path loss this
    change exists to surface just as silent as before. The throttle is what
    keeps level 0 from being noisy, and the two warnings no longer share a
    throttle key: storage_info() calls activate_storage and then status in one
    process, so a shared key let one demote the other to debug.
  * A resolution memo entry that has been failing for a full TTL drops its last
    known addresses instead of keeping them forever, so a portal that changed
    address while the resolver was down stops being matched against an address
    it no longer has.
  * Per-portal backoff and the degraded-redundancy throttle are keyed by
    subsystem rather than by API host, so one storage cannot suppress another
    sharing the same NAS, and both survive a clock step backwards.
  * The redundancy summary recounts live controllers from sysfs instead of
    adding up connect attempts. `nvme connect` returns as soon as the controller
    exists, which may be in connecting, so counting attempts could report all
    portals live with one controller - and suppress the very warning this change
    adds.
  * Repair attempts are rate limited per portal (60s) and the reduced-redundancy
    warning is throttled (300s); a failed connect also drops the target-side
    portal-sync TTL so the next activation re-syncs instead of trusting it.
  * Portals configured by hostname are resolved for key matching only, memoized
    for 300s. The address passed to nvme connect is still the configured one.
  * Add t/nvme/, offline unit tests that build a fixture sysfs tree and need
    neither TrueNAS nor a configured storage.

 -- Alfonso Kuen <alfonsok@gmail.com>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 20:30:00 +0000

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~beta8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * fix(#88): path() fast-fail + deterministic by-path fallback.
    PVE's destroy_vm foreach_volume_full closure at QemuServer.pm:1839
    calls path() -> remove_owned_drive -> vdisk_free -> free_image;
    when path() died, the closure exited before free_image ran and
    TrueNAS state leaked (zvol, iSCSI extent, and target-extent
    mapping stayed behind forever). The old path() called
    _device_for_lun with the default 600-retry budget (60 s), longer
    than the destroy hold, so any node that never activated the disk
    locally (cross-node destroy of an orphan, destroy after a leaked
    config-migrate, or destroy on a node whose iSCSI session was
    down) aborted destroy_vm before free_image ran. Now: retries=5
    (500 ms) at the path() call site, deterministic by-path string
    fallback (/dev/disk/by-path/ip-<portal>-iscsi-<iqn>-lun-<N>) for
    callers that don't need a live device, and die only if we lack
    LUN + portal + IQN to build the deterministic name. Verified with
    a two-scenario timing test: same-node destroy 11.5 s, cross-node
    destroy with iSCSI session logged out on destroying node 15.6 s;
    TN state cleaned in both. Pre-fix the cross-node case hung ~60 s
    and leaked. Closes #88.

  * fix: iSCSI extent blocksize=4096 pblocksize=true to satisfy the
    QEMU 10.1 alignment check. QEMU 10.1's stricter permission/
    alignment negotiation refuses to attach a scsi-hd whose backing
    image reports the mismatch shape that TrueNAS's default extent
    config produces: logical_block_size=512 alongside
    physical_block_size=16384 (inferred from the zvol's 16K
    volblocksize). qm start on any iSCSI-backed disk dies with:
    "kvm: -device scsi-hd,...,write-cache=on: Cannot get 'write'
    permission without 'resize': Image size is not a multiple of
    request alignment". Now every iscsi.extent.create payload
    (_tn_extent_create, _alloc_image_iscsi, _clone_image_iscsi)
    sends blocksize=4096 and pblocksize=true, so TN reports
    lbs=4096 pbs=4096 to the initiator and QEMU's check passes.
    Existing extents are unchanged; older VMs that already fail to
    start under QEMU 10.1 need their extents updated in place or
    refreshed via a clone. Verified: 2G / 3G / 4G disks previously
    refused by qm start now start on the same QEMU/PVE combination.

  * docs(#117): walk through Base Name, Portal, and Target group
    bindings in README.md and wiki/Installation.md. The old iSCSI
    setup recipe covered only the target creation step, producing
    the exact failure mode from issue #117: target created on
    TrueNAS but 'iscsiadm --mode discovery' from Proxmox returned
    empty and the plugin could only get as far as extent creation.
    Both docs now walk through: verify the iSCSI Base Name under
    Target Global Configuration (must start with 'iqn.'; feeds
    tn_target_iqn as '<Base Name>:<Target Name>'); create/verify
    the Portal on 0.0.0.0:3260 and note the Portal ID; create the
    Target with Portal Group ID + Initiator Group ID bindings and
    Authentication Method set explicitly, with a callout that
    without those group bindings the target exists but is not
    exposed on the network. Refs #117.

