truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~beta5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New feature: import foreign TrueNAS snapshots (PR #110 by Alfonso
    Kuen). ZFS snapshots created outside PVE (periodic snapshot tasks,
    replication targets, hand-invoked `zfs snapshot`) are otherwise
    invisible to PVE, but they already affect it: a TrueNAS snapshot
    newer than the last PVE snapshot blocks `qm rollback` with "is not
    most recent snapshot" and rollback destroys them recursively. The
    new `truenas-proxmox-manage import-snapshots <vmid>` walks the
    guest's disks on TrueNAS and adopts the missing snapshots into the
    PVE configuration so they can be listed, rolled back, and deleted
    by the standard `qm listsnapshot` / `qm rollback` / `qm delsnapshot`
    workflow. Explicit, per-guest, with `--dry-run` to preview and
    `--match REGEX` to import only the ones you want. Covers both VMs
    and LXC containers (rootfs plus additional mountpoints); refuses
    containers with bind mounts because PVE itself does. Non-import
    guest state is left alone.

  * Cluster-aware refusal for the importer. `truenas-proxmox-manage
    import-snapshots <vmid>` invoked on a node that does not own the
    guest now dies with an explicit "$vmid is a VM/CT on node 'X', not
    on this one; run import-snapshots there" instead of the generic
    "Configuration file 'nodes/<node>/qemu-server/<vmid>.conf' does
    not exist". Root cause was that _tn_guest_config queried
    PVE::Cluster::get_vmlist() from a fresh Perl process (install.sh's
    `exec perl -MPVE::Storage::Custom::TrueNASPlugin ...` dispatch is
    always a fresh process), where the in-process cluster vmlist cache
    is empty until PVE::Cluster::cfs_update() is called first;
    get_vmlist() returned {ids => {}} regardless of what pmxcfs had
    propagated, and $entry stayed undef.

  * Docs: import-snapshots note in wiki/Tools.md explains that adopted
    snapshots are crash-consistent (no guest-agent fs-freeze, no RAM
    state), unlike PVE-native `qm snapshot` with the guest agent
    enabled; points at wiki/Best-Practices.md for when the distinction
    matters. Also documents `--dry-run` and `--match REGEX` semantics.

 -- Bill O'Hanlon <claude_104@ixsystems.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 14:30:00 -0400

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~beta4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Packaging (debian/postinst): restart pve-ha-crm and pve-ha-lrm on
    install/upgrade (#100).

    PVE::Storage.pm globs /usr/share/perl5/PVE/Storage/Custom/*.pm and
    registers each plugin class exactly once, at the moment a process
    first loads PVE::Storage. pve-ha-crm and pve-ha-lrm auto-start at
    boot whether or not HA is in use, so on a node where they were
    already running before this package was installed, HA-manager
    never learns the truenasplugin type exists until those two
    daemons restart -- surfacing as "storage provided by plugin is
    unsupported" in HA resource config. debian/postinst now restarts
    pve-ha-crm/pve-ha-lrm alongside pvedaemon/pveproxy/pvestatd, still
    gated by the existing TRUENAS_PLUGIN_NO_RESTART opt-out. Verified
    live that a bare service restart never triggers a real HA
    freeze/migrate/fence action, because PVE::HA::LRM only queues
    those on a pending shutdown.target/reboot.target job -- which a
    systemctl restart does not create.

  * Plugin runtime: status() capacity cache now survives across
    processes via a /run/truenas-plugin/status-<key> stamp (issue
    #106).

    status()'s STATUS_CAPACITY_TTL_S=10s cache lived in the in-process
    %API_CACHE. Every `pvesm status` is a fresh Perl interpreter so
    that cache was always cold in exactly the path PVE hits from
    PVE::API2::Storage::Status::upload -- that handler runs
    `ssh peer pvesm status --storage local` synchronously and
    pveproxy closes the connection at 5s idle. Reporter measured
    8-11s per pvesm status on a 501-dataset pool; roughly two of
    three Cluster-API VM uploads failed with "curl: (52) Empty
    reply from server". status() now first checks the in-process
    cache (unchanged), then falls through to an on-disk JSON stamp
    that any process can read within STATUS_CAPACITY_STAMP_TTL_S,
    and only then hits pool.dataset.get_instance. Cache is
    invalidated on any mutation the plugin already invalidates for.
    Stamp I/O is best-effort (bumps a counter but never dies).

    Stamp TTL initially set to 60s, verified by inspection then
    lowered to 15s after disk_discard.pl fill/observe on the test
    cluster came back as "writing data grew the storage allocation:
    +0.00 GiB". 60s TTL was long enough that the after-fill
    pvesm status hit the stamp written before the ~30s in-guest
    fill and returned the pre-fill value. 15s is short enough that
    a ~30s write completes past the TTL, so the follow-up status()
    reflects the fresh 'used' number, while still coalescing the
    burst of back-to-back cross-node upload probes that motivated
    the fix.

  * Plugin runtime + broker: FQDN handling on PVE 9 / Perl 5.40
    (issue #102). Two independent FQDN failures reported against
    a 7-node PVE 9.2 cluster on TrueNAS SCALE 25.10.7:

    - _host_ipv4 called Socket::gethostbyname($host). Perl 5.40
      (Debian Trixie, ships with PVE 9) removed the AUTOLOAD path
      that used to resolve that name to the core builtin; the call
      now dies with "Undefined subroutine Socket::AUTOLOAD" and the
      storage never comes up when tn_api_host is an FQDN. Switched
      both TrueNASPlugin.pm and tools/truenas-plugin-broker to
      Socket::inet_aton, which is the proper Socket export for an
      A-record lookup and works on every supported Perl.

    - _portal_connected compared the configured portal against
      iscsiadm session output as a literal string. iscsiadm always
      reports the resolved IPv4 (e.g. 192.0.2.10:3260), storage.cfg
      often holds the FQDN (e.g. storage.example.com:3260); the two
      never match, so _iscsi_login_all retried login on every
      activate_volume, and open-iscsi answered "1 session
      requested, but 1 already present". Task still completed
      TASK OK but the log noise was on every VM start.
      _portal_connected now precomputes the IP-resolved form of the
      configured portal (via the newly-reliable _host_ipv4) and
      matches session lines against both the original form and the
      IP form. Falls back to the literal path when DNS resolution
      fails; IP literals stay a no-op.

 -- Bill O'Hanlon <claude_104@ixsystems.com>  Thu, 18 Sep 2026 23:15:00 -0400

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~beta3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Bundle of fixes across the plugin runtime, the broker, install.sh,
    and packaging. Every item below cites the GitHub issue it closes
    or contributes to.

  * Plugin runtime (TrueNASPlugin.pm):

    - #77 (WarlockSyno): _api_bulk_call passed 4 args to _api_call_mutate,
      whose 3-arg Perl signature dies before reaching TrueNAS. Trailing
      "die on transport unavailable" fallback was aspirational --
      _api_call_mutate already dies from its own broker path -- so
      dropped the 4th argument. Dormant in shipping releases (dead
      code path) but no longer a trap the moment anything wires the
      bulk helpers in.

    - #76 (WarlockSyno): _wait_for_job_completion called TrueNAS as
      _api_call($scfg, 'core.call', ['core.get_jobs', ...]). TN's
      middleware has no 'core.call' method (JSON-RPC -32601 verified
      live against TN 25.10.2.1), so every job-completion poll
      returned an eval error and the plugin reported success as
      failure. Rewrote to call 'core.get_jobs' directly with the
      standard .query filter shape [[ ["id", "=", int($job_id)] ]].
      Same "dormant in current release paths, but a trap for future
      wiring" impact as #77.

    - #90 (joaquinv98, root-cause traced by WarlockSyno): plugin
      hardcoded allow_any_host => JSON::PP::true on the NVMe subsystem
      in six sites. On TN 25.10.x with a populated allowed_hosts,
      attr_allow_any_host=1 is mutually exclusive with the allow-list
      at the kernel level; nvmet's config writer aborts the whole
      render, port-subsys/port/namespace symlinks never get written,
      the TCP listener never opens, and namespace deletions never
      propagate to the kernel (zvols stay EBUSY, feeds #88 half 2).
      Introduced `tn_nvme_allow_any_host` config knob (bool, optional,
      default TRUE) plus helper `_nvme_allow_any_host_flag($scfg)`
      wiring the value through the subsystem-create call and all five
      Issue-#12 configfs-resync ping sites. Users on TN 25.10.x with
      a populated allowed_hosts should set `tn_nvme_allow_any_host 0`
      in storage.cfg to opt into the enforcement posture and fix the
      reporter's original render-abort bug on that version.

      Default is TRUE (not FALSE, which was tried and reverted during
      beta3 iteration): on TN 26.0.0-BETA.2 a freshly-created subsystem
      with allow_any_host=false AND empty allowed_hosts is silently
      not rendered to the kernel configfs at all -- port has no
      listener, no host can connect, `nvme discover` shows only the
      discovery subsystem. Verified live: flipping a fresh
      allow_any_host=false subsys to true on TN 26.0.0-BETA.2
      immediately triggers configfs render (generation counter
      increments, subsys appears in discovery). Since we cannot
      auto-populate allowed_hosts from the plugin without a bigger
      rework, defaulting to true keeps NVMe working out-of-the-box
      on 26.x and lets 25.10.x users opt into the enforcement posture
      when they have configured allowed_hosts. Auto-population of
      the host allow-list is deferred to a later release.

    - #98 (lubosmikulec): broker's core.ping liveness check was
      no_auth_required, so it kept returning success even after
      TrueNAS silently expired the API-key session at 30 days
      (AA_LEVEL1.max_session_age hardcoded on TN middleware). Broker
      would then indefinitely hand out an unauth'd connection whose
      every real API call fails ENOTAUTHENTICATED; only a
      broker restart recovered. Switched broker liveness check to
      `auth.me` (requires auth). Also extended plugin's preflight
      "API reachable" call from core.ping to auth.me, and extended
      _is_auth_error() to match ENOTAUTHENTICATED / "Not authenticated".

      auth.me returns a large response (full user + role list) that
      TN's middleware serialises around user-record locks. Running
      it on every get_or_open_ws() call as a naive replacement for
      core.ping tripped 596 PVE-API timeouts under multi-node test
      load. The broker now caches the last successful liveness probe
      per pool entry for LIVENESS_CACHE_SECS (60s) and skips the
      probe when it's still fresh -- healthy connections pay near
      zero per-call overhead, and the 30-day expiry is still detected
      within one cache window of the next call. A freshly-opened
      pool entry is stamped as-of the successful ws_open() +
      auth.login_with_api_key, so the first burst of calls after a
      new session doesn't probe redundantly either.

    - #68 (kbogert, root-cause + fix by WarlockSyno): bracketed IPv6
      literals in tn_api_host (e.g. `[fd00:1::1]`) were rejected by
      the storage-config parser, because the schema format was
      `pve-storage-server`, which accepts only bare IPv4/DNS names
      and refuses colons/brackets. Switched to `pve-storage-portal-dns`
      (same format PVE core's ISCSIPlugin uses for its portal field),
      which correctly parses bracketed IPv6. Paired plugin fixes in
      _ws_open: normalise bare IPv6 to bracketed form for consistent
      PeerHost/Host-header/SNI handling, and strip brackets before
      passing to SSL_hostname so SNI + certificate name-verify match
      the cert's IPv6 SAN. Cherry-picked from PR-branch
      fix/issue-68-ipv6-api-host after realising it had never been
      merged to alpha despite the earlier close-invitation.

    - #85 / #84 (JonahMMay): two related template-clone bugs.
      _find_free_disk_name and list_images' owner-VMID detection only
      recognized `vm-<vmid>-...` names, so a template disk already
      renamed to `base-<vmid>-disk-N` by create_base became invisible
      to both the disk allocator and any VMID-scoped listing. Moving
      a second disk of the same template to a storage reused the same
      disk index and create_base's rename crashed with "dataset already
      exists". Both now match `base-`-prefixed names too. Cloud-init
      volumes were named vol-<zname>-lun<N>/-ns<uuid> like every other
      disk, but PVE core's drive_is_cloudinit() only recognizes a
      volid ending in "cloudinit" with no vol- prefix or metadata
      suffix. Since ours never matched, core treated cloud-init drives
      as plain CD-ROMs and copied the reference verbatim on clone
      instead of regenerating it, so clones silently kept the
      template's cloud-init config. Cloud-init disks are now named
      exactly `vm-<vmid>-cloudinit`; since that form can't carry
      embedded LUN/NVMe-UUID metadata, path() and activate_volume
      resolve the transport device dynamically by zvol path when
      metadata is absent. Also carried in this cherry-pick: shared
      `_resolve_iscsi_lun` / `_resolve_nvme_uuid` helpers used from
      path() / activate_volume / free_image; free_image best-effort
      LUN resolve so its pre-teardown SCSI cleanup no longer skips
      cloud-init deletes; NVMe path()'s device-discovery retry now
      short-circuits when the re-resolved UUID matches the failed one
      (avoids a duplicate subsystem reconnect); nvmet.namespace.query
      now cached in _resolve_nvme_uuid to halve back-to-back
      activate_volume + path() round trips. Cherry-picked from PR-branch
      fix/issue-84-85-template-clone-naming after realising it had
      never been merged to alpha.

    - #91 (klosz007): _iscsi_login_all gave tn_portals entries a
      guaranteed --login fallback but not tn_discovery_portal, which
      only got a session if it happened to appear in the node list
      produced by sendtargets discovery. With distinct portals for
      the discovery IP and tn_portals (the documented multipath
      example) only the tn_portals IP connected. The fallback loop
      now covers $primary the same as @extra. Cherry-picked from
      PR-branch fix/issue-91-iscsi-primary-portal-login.

  * Broker (tools/truenas-plugin-broker):

    - #98: paired with the plugin change above. get_or_open_ws()
      pings with auth.me instead of core.ping, so an expired session
      fails the check and the pool entry is dropped + reopened +
      re-auth'd on the next call.

  * install.sh:

    - #68 (kbogert, root-cause + fix by WarlockSyno): matching
      installer-side IPv6 gaps. validate_ip() accepts IPv6 bare or
      bracketed. validate_host() routes any colon-containing input
      to validate_ip() instead of the hostname regex. /dev/tcp
      reachability probes strip brackets before connecting (bash
      doesn't understand bracket syntax). Manual portal IP:port
      entry now disambiguates [ipv6]:port, bare IPv6, and
      ipv4-or-host:port as three separate forms. TrueNAS interface
      auto-discovery captures IPv6 addresses too (was IPv4-only) and
      skips ::1/fe80:: link-local. tn_create_portal /
      tn_create_nvme_port / tn_find_nvme_port strip brackets before
      sending to TrueNAS APIs (which expect bare addresses).


    - #67 (spz-uquidit): plugin function test hardcoded --net0
      bridge=vmbr0 in three qm create sites, failing outright on
      hosts without vmbr0 (common on hosts with separate storage
      and public bridges). New `test_pick_bridge()` helper resolves
      bridge via env var override (TRUENAS_PLUGIN_TEST_BRIDGE),
      then vmbr0 if it exists, then first vmbr* found via
      `ip -o link show type bridge`, falling back to vmbr0 to
      preserve legacy behaviour. All three test-VM create sites
      now use the helper.

    - #86 issue 4 (RobD68): normalize_tag_version() regex accepted
      only vX.Y.Z or vX.Y.Z-debN, so any alpha/beta/rc release tag
      (v2.1.21-alpha1, v2.1.23-beta2) was rejected with "Release
      tag format is invalid; expected vX.Y.Z or vX.Y.Z-debN",
      forcing admins to dpkg -i a manually-downloaded .deb. Broadened
      the pre-release suffix regex to `(-[A-Za-z0-9._~+-]+)?$`.

    - #86 issue 1 (plappermaul): execute_provisioning() silently
      accepted an empty PROV_SUBSYSTEM_ID from a
      `python3 -c "...data.get('id', '')"` one-liner with stderr
      suppressed, then skipped the NVMe port block (gated on
      -n PROV_SUBSYSTEM_ID) without incrementing errors, leaving
      PROVISIONED_PORTAL_IP unset and writing a literal
      `tn_discovery_portal :` (bare colon) to storage.cfg. Now
      captures the underlying lookup's exit code and stderr, prints
      a loud error with remediation guidance, and increments errors
      so the errors gate on the port block does its job.

  * Packaging (debian/):

    - #60 (lucidnx): postinst unconditionally ran `systemctl
      try-restart pvedaemon pveproxy pvestatd`, silently disconnecting
      any active PVE web-UI session (painful for admins reaching
      the UI through jump hosts or PAM-mediated password vaults).
      Added TRUENAS_PLUGIN_NO_RESTART=1 env-var escape hatch: when
      set, postinst skips the PVE-side restart and prints the exact
      command to run manually. truenas-plugin-broker restart in
      enable_broker() still runs unconditionally (our own service,
      does not touch the web UI).

    - #4 (JonahMMay): Ship a new /usr/sbin/truenas-plugin-lvm-filter
      helper that appends a global_filter reject regex to
      /etc/lvm/lvm.conf's existing list, addressing the host-side
      LVM scanning of TN NVMe/TCP namespaces (which triggers
      duplicate-VG warning storms and can cascade into 596s API
      timeouts that break Veeam and other pollers). The regex
      matches `/dev/disk/by-id/nvme-TrueNAS_.*`, populated by udev
      from the NVMe controller MODEL string; local NVMe drives use
      other vendor prefixes and are untouched. Not applied at
      package install (that would silently edit pve-manager's
      conffile); the sysadmin runs `truenas-plugin-lvm-filter
      --install` explicitly. Idempotent, with timestamped backup
      and clean `--uninstall`. Documented in
      wiki/LVM-Filter.md, cross-linked from Troubleshooting.md.

  * No functional change over #77 and #76's shipping behaviour
    (both were latent code paths, not user-visible bugs). #90 is
    the only semi-breaking change and is scoped by the migration
    notes above. #98 removes the 30-day session-expiry silent
    failure. #60/#67/#86/#4 are all admin-experience improvements
    with well-defined defaults.

