truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha36) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe: reap orphan namespaces on our subsystem after every free_image
    and before every emergency reconnect.

    Under multi-node concurrent load a namespace teardown occasionally
    fails partway (network glitch, TN transient). The plugin logs a
    warn but has no persistent retry ledger, so the namespace lives on
    TN forever. Test framework never re-runs delete for that VM because
    from its perspective the VM is gone. Over many operations these
    orphans accumulate on TN. The publication-mismatch detector in
    _nvme_device_for_uuid (added in alphas 20-30) then reports
      TrueNAS API namespace count: 49
      Linux-visible block devices: 11
      Selector mismatch reason: namespace_metadata_did_not_match_visible_devices
    and dies for every activate_volume that runs against the ballooned
    namespace list. This is exactly the "chug along fine then upset"
    pattern Max R. Carrara flagged in his 2026-08-27 test_run7 mail --
    253-274 mismatch cascades per 3-node cluster subrun, with TN's
    namespace count growing monotonically (4 -> 8 -> 25 -> 49 in one
    subrun) while Linux only ever saw 7-11 devices.

    Fix: new helper _nvme_reap_orphan_namespaces that queries
    nvmet.namespace on our subsystem, cross-checks each namespace's
    device_path against a fresh pool.dataset.query for datasets under
    our tn_dataset prefix, and deletes any namespace whose backing
    dataset no longer exists. Scope is limited to our subsystem's
    slice under our storage's dataset prefix; namespaces belonging to
    other storage configs sharing the same subsystem, and ephemeral
    vzdump snapshot clones (issue #42), are left alone.

    Called from two places:
      1. _free_image_nvme's _defer_after_lock block. Runs off the
         caller's synchronous path after every namespace free, so
         orphans get cleaned up at the natural rate of one-per-free
         without slowing new-VM creation.
      2. _nvme_device_for_uuid's emergency-reconnect branch, before
         firing the reconnect. Reconnecting the kernel does not shrink
         an orphan pile on TN; TN still publishes the ballooned
         namespace list and the next device lookup can still mismatch.
         Reap first, then reconnect -- the reconnect's re-enumeration
         then reflects the trimmed namespace set.

    Two extra TN API calls per invocation (nvmet.namespace.query +
    pool.dataset.query on our subsystem+prefix). Both are already
    called in adjacent code paths; the incremental cost is small
    compared to the failure mode it prevents. Best-effort throughout
    -- never dies; if TN itself is unreachable we log and return.

    NVMe reap only. The iSCSI equivalent -- iscsi.extent.query
    cross-checked with pool.dataset.query -- is a smaller problem
    because the extent-delete path in _free_image_iscsi already runs
    synchronously and the extent name uniqueness constraint stops
    orphan accumulation from becoming self-reinforcing. Future work
    if the same shape shows up on iSCSI under similar load.

 -- Bill O'Hanlon <claude_104@ixsystems.com>  Wed, 27 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0000

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha35) unstable; urgency=medium

  * create_base: on-EEXIST recovery for the pool.dataset.rename step.

    TN raises
      [EEXIST] zfs.resource.rename: 'tank/<pool>/base-<vmid>-disk-N' already exists
    when the target base dataset is left over from a prior template of the
    same VMID that was not fully cleaned. Before this change the plugin
    caught the error, re-enabled the NVMe namespace on that branch, and
    rethrew -- the caller's linked-clone step then failed as a cascade
    ("template task succeeded" -> "template / linked-clone: Linked clone
    feature is not supported for vol-vm-*").

    Fix: on that specific error shape, query the leftover base dataset for
    non-snapshot children and for linked clones deriving from its
    @__base__ snapshot (any pool.dataset whose origin.parsed points at
    <target>@__base__ is a live clone). If both are empty, delete the
    leftover with recursive=true+force=true and retry the rename once.
    If either check finds live data, fall through to the original
    validation error so the operator sees the real conflict rather than
    a silent redirect.

    Impact observed: 9-54 hits per node in Max R. Carrara's test_run7
    2026-08-25 3-node cluster runs. The affected test file
    (template_linked_clone.pl) is the only path in the suite that
    exercises create_base under multi-round load; every hit produced
    both a "template task succeeded" failure and a downstream
    "Linked clone feature is not supported" failure. With the recovery
    in place the rename retries transparently and the linked-clone step
    proceeds.

  * list_images: bypass the per-worker extents / targetextents cache in the
    iSCSI implementation.

    The disk_purge test asserts that a purged volid disappears from the
    storage content listing. It failed intermittently even when the
    delete succeeded on TN, because:
      1. free_image ran in pvedaemon worker A, deleted the iSCSI extent
         and targetextent synchronously, cleared worker A's per-process
         _tn_extents / _tn_targetextents cache, and returned a coderef.
      2. PVE forked the coderef as an imgdel UPID task (the
         pool.dataset.delete is intentionally deferred to keep the
         caller's storage lock hold under the 180s test-framework limit
         that vm_disk_buses breached in test_run6/truenas-2026-08-13).
      3. The test's next call landed on worker B, whose per-process
         cache still had the deleted extent + mapping.
      4. The existing ground-truth check at line 6495
         (`next MAPPING if $datasets_ok && !exists $dataset_cache{$ds_full}`)
         could not filter it out because the dataset was genuinely still
         on TN -- the imgdel task had not yet run pool.dataset.delete.
      5. Volume showed up in the listing -> test failed.

    Fix: in _list_images_iscsi replace `_tn_extents($scfg)` /
    `_tn_targetextents($scfg)` with direct `_api_call($scfg,
    'iscsi.extent.query', [])` and `_api_call($scfg,
    'iscsi.targetextent.query', [])`. Fresh reads mean the extent-based
    iteration no longer surfaces the phantom regardless of which worker
    handles the request. Two extra TN API calls per list_images
    invocation; list_images is not in a hot loop -- it's called on
    demand from storage content queries.

    NVMe is not affected: _list_images_nvme already reads namespaces
    directly via `nvmet.namespace.query` (line 6604) without going
    through a cached wrapper.

  * broker: downgrade the "upstream error" WARN to INFO for the well-known
    "already gone" delete race.

    Any DELETE method (iscsi.extent.delete, iscsi.targetextent.delete,
    pool.dataset.delete, nvmet.namespace.delete, ...) that TN answers
    with InstanceNotFound / ENOENT / "does not exist" is a benign race
    the plugin's callers already handle idempotently (see e.g.
    free_image, _teardown_snapshot_device). Leaving those at WARN level
    drowned real errors under vzdump-* ephemeral snapshot-clone
    teardown noise: Max R. Carrara's test_run7 2026-08-25 3-node
    cluster-01 logged around 2000 of these per node per subrun.

    Fix: in the broker's structured-error handler downgrade to INFO
    (`upstream ENOENT on <method> (already gone; sk=<sk>)`) when
    the method name ends in .delete AND the error carries
    InstanceNotFound / \bENOENT\b / "does not exist". All other error
    shapes (rename EEXIST, validation errors, EBUSY, rate limit, ...)
    still log at WARN. Cosmetic on correctness but restores
    signal-to-noise ratio for future forensics.

 -- Bill O'Hanlon <claude_104@ixsystems.com>  Wed, 27 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha34) unstable; urgency=medium

  * iSCSI: fix create_base extent-rename gap that produced cascading
    "iscsi_extent_create.name: Extent name must be unique" failures on
    every VM allocation at a VMID that had previously been templated.

    Root cause: create_base renamed the zvol vm-<vmid>-disk-N ->
    base-<vmid>-disk-N and issued iscsi.extent.update to rewrite the
    extent's `disk` field, but never renamed the extent itself. The
    vm-<vmid>-disk-N-<hash> extent-name slot on TN stayed owned by
    the (now-base) extent. _generate_extent_name is deterministic on
    (dataset, zname), so the next VM allocated at the same VMID hashed
    to the same name and collided. Fix B looked up the conflicting
    extent by name, saw its disk field pointed at a base zvol (not
    ours), and correctly refused to reuse -- there was no safe reuse
    path, and no repair path either. Test file died, next test file
    hit it too, cascade.

    Impact observed:
      - Max R. Carrara's test_run6/truenas-2026-08-13 3-node cluster
        runs: 2348-2632 "Extent name must be unique" hits per node.
      - Same runs single-node: 1180 hits (visible even without
        multi-node concurrency).
      - Our iSCSI-only run 2026-08-24 against alpha33: 128-199 per
        node, all on the same extent id.
      - Was masked in earlier report summaries as generic FAIL verdicts
        spread across ~14 different capability tests
        (storage_plugin_disk_purge, template_linked_clone,
        vm_create_default, etc.) -- not clustered as one bug.

    Fix has two parts:

    Part 1 (prevent new occurrences). create_base's iSCSI branch now
    includes {name => _generate_extent_name($new_zname)} in the
    iscsi.extent.update payload alongside the existing {disk =>
    $new_zvol_path}. New templates leave TN with a properly-named
    base-*-<hash> extent, freeing the vm-*-<hash> slot for future VMs.

    Part 2 (recover from historical TN state). New helper
    _iscsi_extent_recover_stale_base_name detects the shape of a
    stale-extent conflict (same name, disk field points at a
    base-<vmid>-disk-N zvol) and renames the extent to its proper
    base-*-<hash> name. All three Fix B call sites -- _tn_extent_create,
    _alloc_image_iscsi, _clone_image_iscsi -- invoke the helper in the
    "refusing to reuse" branch and retry the create once on success.

    The rename is bookkeeping-only from the initiator side: extent_id,
    targetextent mapping, and naa/serial identifiers are all preserved.
    Nothing on the wire changes; only the TN-side extent name is
    corrected.

    NVMe is not affected: nvmet namespaces are keyed by (subsys, nsid),
    not by a name attribute, so the equivalent create_base rewire (of
    namespace.device_path) does not leave a stale name slot behind.

