truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha30) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe: avoid nsid recycling on namespace create.

    Alpha29's pre-wait subsys.update didn't stop the failure. Deep
    inspection showed the ACTUAL disagreement between TN and kernel:
      kernel: nvme0n3 nsid=3 uuid=<OLD-DELETED-NAMESPACE-UUID>
      TN DB : nsid=3 uuid=<NEW-NAMESPACE-UUID>
    TN's nvmet.namespace.create auto-assigns the lowest free nsid.
    Under multi-node create/destroy churn nsids recycle rapidly.
    TN's configfs sync from DB does not always land the nsid=3
    rewrite before a connecting host reads the port's namespace list.
    Kernel then has a stale UUID at that nsid and no rescan, subsys
    reload, or full disconnect+reconnect can dislodge it — every
    such op re-reads TN configfs, which still shows the stale entry.

    Fix: explicit monotonic nsid on create. Query current max nsid
    for the subsystem, add random jitter 1..8 (reduces cross-node
    concurrent-alloc collisions), pass explicit nsid in the create
    payload. Every namespace lands at a nsid that has never been
    used in this subsystem's lifetime — kernel has no cached mapping
    for that nsid, so it enumerates fresh from configfs. Configfs
    for a never-used nsid can't be stale.

    Alpha30 falls back to TN auto-assignment when max_nsid + jitter
    approaches the subsystem NN ceiling (1024). Under Max's test-suite
    churn this ceiling is well above what we see (peak ~20 concurrent
    namespaces).

    Also handles the rare cross-node "both nodes picked the same
    max+jitter" collision by re-querying max and retrying once with
    a fresh jitter.

 -- Bill O'Hanlon <claude_104@ixsystems.com>  Fri, 21 Aug 2026 19:00:00 +0000

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha29) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe: activate_volume forces nvmet.subsys.update before waiting.

    Alpha28's emergency reconnect fired correctly on the failures but
    always logged:
      "emergency reconnect completed but target UUID still not visible
       after 2s — falling through to error"
    Even a fresh disconnect+reconnect couldn't make the kernel see the
    target namespace. That ruled out kernel-side stale cache. Root
    cause is on TrueNAS: nvmet.namespace.create returns as soon as
    the DB row lands, BEFORE the configfs entry under
    /sys/kernel/config/nvmet/subsystems/*/namespaces/* is created.
    An nvmet.subsys.update forces TN to flush DB->configfs — the
    plugin has this workaround, but it runs in _defer_after_lock
    inside alloc_image/clone_image/expose_snapshot, only on the
    CREATING node.

    Under multi-node clustering where VM allocation happens on one
    node and start (activate_volume) happens on another (cluster
    migration, live migrate, or PVE routing an allocate to node A
    and a start to node B), the DIFFERENT node's activate never runs
    the workaround. It connects, sees pre-namespace configfs state,
    and no rescan/reconnect can invent an entry TN never wrote to
    configfs.

    Fix: activate_volume's NVMe branch fires nvmet.subsys.update
    itself, right after _nvme_connect and before _nvme_device_for_uuid.
    Idempotent, one extra API call per activation, only when the
    plugin is about to wait for a device. Forces TN to sync its
    configfs so the subsequent device-lookup can succeed.

