truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha23) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe: restructure the publication-mismatch check for shared subsystems.

    Alpha22 tightened rescan cadence and doubled the budget. Helped
    the short bursts, but under sustained 3-node concurrent creates
    the "Could not locate NVMe device" failure came back at scale
    (15 mismatches on pve, 13 on proxmox2, 13 on pvevm1 across ~90
    tests). Root cause: the check was structurally wrong for shared
    NVMe-oF subsystems.

    - Every node connected to a shared subsystem enumerates its OWN
      view of published namespaces. TN's API returns the TOTAL for
      the subsystem, which includes namespaces every other node just
      created. Kernel-count strictly less-than api-count is the
      STEADY-STATE norm in this environment, not a fault.
    - The old 'linux_api_namespace_count_mismatch' reason flagged
      the norm as a fault. Once the retry loop exhausted its budget,
      that fault was the exit reason.

    Changes:
      1. Dropped 'linux_api_namespace_count_mismatch' from the reason
         table. Count inequality no longer produces a diagnostic
         "mismatch" — only "api_namespace_not_visible_in_linux" (0
         kernel devices) and "single_visible_device_but_api_reports_
         multiple_namespaces" (stale-device suspect) remain as real
         faults.
      2. Iteration budget 100 → 150 (10s → 15s). Still well under
         pveproxy's 60s cap.
      3. New halfway-point (i==75) reconnect trigger for the exact
         failure mode we observed: target UUID confirmed on TN but
         this host's kernel hasn't seen it yet after ~7.5s. Fires
         disconnect+reconnect ONLY when no subsystem device is in
         use by any process on this host (fuser check) — reconnect
         momentarily drops all namespaces and would hang running
         VMs writing to other namespaces on the same subsystem.
         Gated on allow_reconnect=1 (already the default from
         activate_volume).

