truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha21) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe: fix retry-storm namespace duplication.

    Root cause: nvmet.namespace.create is not idempotent — the payload
    has no name/key uniqueness on the TN side (unlike iscsi.extent.create
    which is protected by an "Extent name must be unique" constraint
    and the plugin's Fix B name-conflict recovery). Every mutation goes
    through _api_call -> _retry_with_backoff, which retries up to 3
    times on connection errors. Under 3-node concurrent alloc load,
    connection blips are frequent. When TN successfully creates a
    namespace but the WS response is lost mid-flight, the retry fires
    a SECOND create for the same device_path. TN accepts it. Both
    survive.

    Impact observed (2026-08-20 3-node NVMe run, tn-reset log):
      ns_deleted=68 ds_deleted=24  (2.83 namespace rows per zvol)
      total on TN = 96, only 9 published to kernel controllers,
      activate_volume dies with "namespace publication mismatch"
      (linux visible = 9, api reports = 96).

    Fix: new helper _nvme_create_namespace_idempotent that
      (a) queries device_path first — if a namespace exists, reuse it
          (also closes _clone_image_nvme's pre-existing gap where it
          had no existing-check at all — concurrent clones on the
          same target zvol would leak duplicates).
      (b) attempts create with retry_max=0 (retries disabled)
      (c) on any error, re-queries device_path — if a namespace now
          exists, treat as success (create landed before response was
          lost)
      (d) only re-raises when nothing exists after the re-query.

    Applied to all 3 nvmet.namespace.create call sites:
      _alloc_image_nvme (5361), _expose_snapshot_device (6845),
      _clone_image_nvme (7404).

    Zvol-visibility polling loops around each site stay — that error
    is not a connection error and never triggered the outer retry.

 -- Bill O'Hanlon <claude_104@ixsystems.com>  Fri, 21 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha20) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe: fix nvmet.namespace.query filter to use "subsys.id" instead
    of "subsys" (TrueNASPlugin.pm:4014-4020).

    TrueNAS 25.10+ returns each namespace's `subsys` field as a nested
    object ({id, name, subnqn, ...}), not a scalar id. The plugin's
    filter ["subsys", "=", $subsys_id] compared the whole object to
    a scalar and always returned 0. That made api_namespace_count = 0,
    which the publication-mismatch check compared against
    linux_device_count > 0 and failed with:

      TrueNAS namespace publication mismatch detected.
        -> Linux-visible block devices: N
        -> TrueNAS API namespace count: 0
        -> Selector mismatch reason: linux_api_namespace_count_mismatch

    Every NVMe activate_volume died with "Could not locate NVMe device
    for TrueNAS UUID ...". Filter now uses "subsys.id" nested-field
    syntax which returns the correct count on 25.10+.

    Verified against live TN 25.10: [["subsys","=",1]] -> 0 results;
    [["subsys.id","=",1]] -> 14 results (all namespaces).

 -- Bill O'Hanlon <claude_104@ixsystems.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 16:30:00 +0000

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha19) unstable; urgency=medium

  * activate_volume iSCSI: verify size > 0 on the sd backing the LUN
    after rescan; if stuck at 0, purge the stale sd and force session
    --rescan so the kernel re-attaches a live device.

    Symptom (cluster_test_run 2026-08-19):
      io_integrity.pl: short write to
        /dev/disk/by-path/...iqn.2005-10.org.freenas.ctl:proxmox-lun-7
      syswrite returned undef (EIO); test bailed.

    Root cause: under 3-node concurrent load with recycled LUNs
    (test_A destroys VM 105 -> LUN 6 unmapped -> test_B creates new
    VM 106 -> TN reassigns LUN 6 -> new zvol), the kernel keeps sdX
    bound to LUN 6 but drops its READ CAPACITY to 0. The existing
    _iscsi_rescan_sd_capacity pokes /sys/block/sdX/device/rescan but
    does not wait for the result. If capacity is still 0 when
    activate_volume returns, the caller opens a zero-length block
    device and every write fails.

    Fix: new helper _iscsi_ensure_lun_ready(scfg, lun, path) called
    from activate_volume right after _device_for_lun. Retries the
    per-sd rescan up to 10x (300ms sleep, ~3s worst case). If size
    is still 0, writes 1 to /sys/block/sdX/device/delete to evict
    the stale device, runs iscsiadm -m session --rescan to force
    re-attach, re-resolves the by-path symlink, and requires the
    new sd to report size > 0 or dies loud.

    Doesn't touch happy path (size > 0 on first read = zero-cost
    return). Only kicks in on the recycle-race failure mode.

