truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.23~alpha18) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Bypass the PVE CFS cluster storage lock by default. This is the
    single biggest lever for the multi-node concurrent-alloc problem
    the last dozen alphas have been chipping at. cluster_test_run
    2026-08-17 3-node VM 276 timing:

      13:20:30 pvedaemon logs "update VM 276: -scsi1"
      13:22:22 alloc_image FINALLY enters -- ~112 s of pure lock wait
      13:22:40 alloc_image body done in 18 s

    Body time is fine. The 2-minute wait is entirely in
    PVE::Storage::Plugin::cluster_lock_storage's cfs_lock_storage
    serialization while OTHER nodes' allocs held the lock. Test client
    bailed at 60 s with 596; downstream tests then failed on stale
    VM-config locks (test cascade C).

    PVE's cfs_lock_storage is the right primitive for local storages
    (local, LVM) where concurrent PVE-side metadata mutation would
    corrupt state. For a network storage like TrueNAS SCALE it's
    redundant: TN's middleware already serializes conflicting mutations
    (iscsi.extent.create name uniqueness, iscsi.targetextent LUN
    assignment, ZFS dataset name uniqueness), and the plugin's Fix 1
    (alloc-time extent reuse), Fix B (post-hoc name-conflict recovery),
    and the dataset-already-exists auto-increment cover any residual
    PVE-side race. Bypass eliminates the cross-node queue entirely;
    nodes alloc in true parallel, TN serializes internally as needed.

    New tn_use_cluster_lock=1 storage.cfg option restores the classic
    PVE-serialized behavior for regression comparison or for setups
    running an unusual TN configuration where its own internal
    serialization is not trusted. Default remains bypass.

 -- TrueNAS Proxmox Plugin Maintainers <noreply@github.com>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 19:00:00 +0000

