truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.21~alpha2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Untaint TrueNAS API scalars in _normalize_value (fixes disk-migration
    regression seen in test_run5/truenas-2026-07-22, "Insecure dependency
    in exec while running with -T switch at IPC/Open3.pm line 176"). Ports
    the fix from #71 that never landed on the alpha lineage. Every value
    decoded from a WS/broker read is tainted under -T, so volsize would
    flow tainted into qemu-img create argv during any move-disk to a
    non-TrueNAS target. Untaint via regex capture in the single funnel
    point; die on non-numeric input instead of silently forwarding.
  * Retry iscsi.extent.create and nvmet.namespace.create on the specific
    zvol-not-ready validator errors (fixes the 1/20 linked-clone failure
    in test_run5). pool.snapshot.clone / pool.dataset.create returns as
    soon as ZFS is done, but TN validates the create call by stat'ing
    /dev/zvol/<ds>, and udev may still be materializing the symlink.
    New narrow classifier _is_zvol_not_ready_error matches only the two
    validator strings so genuinely bad paths still fail immediately. Up
    to 15 x 200 ms wait applied at every live create site: _tn_extent_create,
    alloc_image (iSCSI + NVMe), _expose_snapshot_device NVMe, and both
    _clone_image_* paths.
  * Broker URL construction correctness: mirror TrueNASPlugin.pm's scheme
    normalization (https -> wss, http -> ws) and honor prefer_ipv4 via a
    new _host_ipv4 helper. Prior broker took api_scheme raw, so a config
    with tn_api_scheme=https would silently fall through to plain TCP and
    the WS handshake would 400; and although the wire schema always
    listed prefer_ipv4 the broker never read it, so the OS resolver could
    pick AAAA on a dual-stack TN even after the plugin resolved A.

 -- TrueNAS Proxmox Plugin Maintainers <noreply@github.com>  Thu, 23 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.22~alpha1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NVMe-oF: add host-whitelist + DH-CHAP support (tn_nvme_allow_any_host).
    The plugin previously hardcoded allow_any_host=true in five places and
    never registered host records, so it forced open access and DH-CHAP keys
    were only ever applied to the initiator (never enforced by the target).
    On TrueNAS 26 this actively broke NVMe: the kernel refuses to set
    allow_any_host=1 on a subsystem that has explicit host links, so the
    nvmet_kernel render aborted (OSError EINVAL on attr_allow_any_host) and
    newly created namespaces never reached the running kernel target -- disk
    creation appeared to succeed but the device was never exposed. New
    tn_nvme_allow_any_host (default 1 = open, back-compat) opts into whitelist
    mode when set to 0: the plugin registers each node's host NQN (with the
    configured DH-CHAP keys) via nvmet.host + nvmet.host_subsys and sets
    allow_any_host=false, registering the host before tightening access so
    live connections are not dropped. The four allow_any_host reapply sites
    now honour the configured value instead of forcing true.
  * NVMe-oF: fix false zero namespace count from a server-side subsys filter
    (cherry-picked). _nvme_get_namespace_selector_metadata counted namespaces
    with [["subsys","=",id]], which matches nothing when TrueNAS returns
    subsys as a nested object -- tripping linux_api_namespace_count_mismatch
    and failing path()/activate on 26. Now counted client-side via a shared
    _nvme_namespace_subsys_id helper accepting either shape.
  * pool.dataset.create: send volblocksize, snapdev, reservation,
    refreservation, special_small_block_size, and force_size explicitly at
    both create sites (#58, #65, #78). TrueNAS 25.10.4's legacy shim left
    omitted optional fields as unresolved _NotRequired sentinels, crashing
    create in validation or audit-log serialization; verified accepted on
    TrueNAS 26.

